\fs16 Hi, I'm developing [serializejson](https://smartaudiotools.github.io/serializejson), a python library for fast serialization and deserialization of complex Python objects in [JSON](http://json.org) highly compatible with [pickle](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\par
I use blosc in order to compress binary data before encoding in Base64 for json serialisation.\par
I need to have always the same json when I serialize same data for comparison, checking and versioning of jsons.\par
Sadly, when multi-threaded , the blosc compression is not determinist for big data, forcing me to use only one thread for compression and losing blosc's advantage over other libraries when compressing. \par
for example, following code give an AssertionError when nb_thread > 1\par
    import blosc\par
    datas = bytes(range(256)) * 1200\par
    nb_thread  = 2\par
    blosc.set_nthreads(nb_thread)\par
    for i in range(20):\par
        compressed_blosc_1 = blosc.compress(datas , 1, cname="zstd", clevel=1)\par
        compressed_blosc_2 = blosc.compress(datas , 1, cname="zstd", clevel=1)\par
        assert compressed_blosc_1 == compressed_blosc_2\par
Is there a possibility of using blosc in multi-tread while maintaining deterministic behavior?\par
Otherwise i would like to use the  [python-zstandard](https://github.com/indygreg/python-zstandard) libray for compression wich is determinist even in multi-thread. Sadly your blosc library wich is much faster than python-zstandard for decompression is not able to decompress data compressed with python-zstandard library beacause of  unrecognised header. \par
Is there an option to recognize or ignore the header of the data compressed with the python-zstandard library if we pass the name of the compression to blosc.decompress ? \par
More generally, is it possible to have data compressed with blosc that does not use specific blosc headers, to have data that can still be readable in 10 years even if blosc is no longer maintained?\par
--------------------\par
Hi. {\cf5 Unfortunately it is not possible}{\cf6  [D\'c9MENTI le 03/08/2026 : le patch de 184 lignes (blosc2_determinisme.patch, commit ordonn\'e9 par variable de condition) rend le MT interne d\'e9terministe sans perte mesurable \'97 ce que l'auteur tenait pour impossible]}. Blosc achieves efficient paralellism by letting the first thread that ends its part of the compression to write first in the output buffer, then the next thread to finish will write after that, and so on so forth; this is the way we can save precious copy time. \cf4 As there is no guarantee on the order of the sequence of completing times for every thread, it is essentially impossible to guarantee an order (except for the trivial case that you use 1 single thread, as you found out already).\par
\cf0\par
Regarding compatibility with zstandard, unfortunately the answer is negative again. Blosc is all about speed, and it makes a number of choices for compressing/decompressing that makes it difficult (if not impossible) to read other formats in order to decompress them faster than the original implementation. At any rate, my guess is that it is going to be difficult for any codec to decompress faster than zstandard when it is required to use zstandard own format.\par
\par
Regarding your last question, well, the Blosc format is completely open (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/Blosc/c-blosc/blob/master/README_CHUNK_FORMAT.rst"}}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf3 https://github.com/Blosc/c-blosc/blob/master/README_CHUNK_FORMAT.rst}}}\f0\fs16 ) so the idea is that, even if Blosc is going to be unmaintained in 10 years, anybody could produce a reader (even a writer!) for the format. In any case, the Blosc team is confident that happy users will provide resources for, hopefully, at least 10 more years.\par
