\fs16 Yes, I know what you was speaking about ;-). Actually, there are a lot of things that differ in the why that blosc+zstd and plain zstd store the output. For one thing, I see that \ul you are directing blosc to use the shuffle filter\ulnone , and I don't think python-zstandard activates this. Then, \ul blosc tends to split the inputs in smaller pieces\ulnone , and then again, this is not something that python-zstandard does. Really, \ul even if blosc and zstd both use the same codec internally, a lot is changing on how the output is created.\ulnone\par
